IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `wim-dates`
IMPORT `wim-claims`

-- Workplace Injury Management and Workers Compensation Act 1998 (NSW) -- when the worker disagrees: the insurer's
-- review, taking a dispute to the Personal Injury Commission, interim payment directions, the medical assessment of
-- permanent impairment and its appeal, the appeal from a Commission decision, and suing the employer for work injury
-- damages (pre-filing statement, response, mediation).
--
-- SEAT: the injured worker (the claimant). The insurer, the defendant employer, the President, the Commission, its
-- medical assessors and the court get no seat: what they decide or are satisfied of is asked as a fact.
--
-- Provisions: s 287A; ss 288(1), 289, 289A, 289B; s 297(2)-(4) with clause 42 of the Workers Compensation Regulation
-- 2016; s 298; s 304; s 308(1), (2); s 280B; ss 313, 314; s 315(2); s 316 (a regulative rule); s 318(1)(c); s 318A(1),
-- (2), (4); s 322A; s 323(1), (2); s 327(3), (5), (7); s 330(2), (3); s 352(3), (3A), (4).
--
-- ANSWERS as in wim-claims.l4: JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the text says no, NOTHING (with its NOTHING-kind) where it does
-- not answer. "Does section X stop me ...?" answers only for that section.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Disputes and work injury damages under the Workplace Injury Management and Workers Compensation Act 1998`

§§ `Asking the insurer to review its decision (s 287A)`

-- s 287A(1) "A worker may request an insurer to review -- (a) a work capacity decision of the insurer that is disputed
-- by the worker, or (b) a claim, or any aspect of a claim, that is disputed by the insurer." (2) "at any time before the
-- dispute is referred for determination by the Commission." (3) the insurer must "notify the worker of the decision on
-- the review, within 14 days after the request is made."
@export May I ask the insurer to review its decision (s 287A(1), (2))?
GIVEN `it is a work capacity decision I dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you dispute a work capacity decision of the insurer (about your capacity, suitable work or earnings)
      `the insurer disputes my claim or part of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has disputed your claim or any aspect of it
      `the dispute has been referred to the Commission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the dispute has already been referred to the Personal Injury Commission for determination
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I ask for an internal review` MEANS
    BRANCH IF    `it is a work capacity decision I dispute`
              OR `the insurer disputes my claim or part of it`
           THEN IF `the dispute has been referred to the Commission`
                THEN JUST FALSE
                ELSE JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 287A(1) lists what may be reviewed)

@export By when must the insurer tell me its review decision (s 287A(3))?
GIVEN `the day I asked for the review` IS A DATE @desc The day you made your written request for review
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I get the review decision` MEANS `the date` 14 `days after` `the day I asked for the review`

§§ `Taking a dispute to the Personal Injury Commission (ss 288-289B)`

DECLARE `Kind of dispute` IS ONE OF
    `weekly payments`
    `weekly payments, on a work capacity decision`
    `medical expenses`
    `whether proposed treatment is reasonably necessary`
    `lump sum compensation`
    `property damage`

-- s 289(1) weekly payments (other than on a work capacity decision) and (2) medical expenses: only if the insurer
-- "(a) disputes liability for the claim (wholly or in part), or (b) fails to determine the claim as and when required";
-- (2A) a dispute whether proposed treatment is reasonably necessary is not held back; (3) lump sum: only if it "(a)
-- wholly disputes liability ..., or (b) made an offer of settlement ... and 1 month has elapsed since the offer was made,
-- or (c) fails to determine the claim"; (4) property damage: "until -- (a) 28 days after the claim ... is made, or (b) the
-- person ... disputes liability ..., whichever happens first". s 289A(1) "only matters previously notified as disputed"
-- (or raised in writing about a lump sum offer: (2)(b)); (4) unless the Commission thinks it in the interests of justice.
-- s 289A is not applied where the only route is a failure to determine, which has no notice of dispute (fork F9).
-- s 288(1): for a lump sum dispute "only the claimant can refer" -- the seat is the claimant.
@export May I refer my dispute to the Personal Injury Commission now (ss 288, 289, 289A)?
GIVEN `the kind of dispute` IS A `Kind of dispute` @desc What the dispute is about: weekly payments; weekly payments, on a work capacity decision; medical expenses; whether proposed treatment is reasonably necessary; lump sum compensation; or property damage
      `the insurer wholly disputes liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has disputed liability for the whole claim
      `the insurer disputes part of liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has disputed liability for part of the claim
      `the insurer has not decided my claim in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has failed to determine your claim as and when the Act requires (for a lump sum claim, this includes not making a reasonable offer of settlement)
      `the insurer made me an offer of settlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a lump sum claim: whether the insurer made you an offer of settlement when it determined the claim
      `the day of the offer` IS A DATE @desc The day of that offer (any day if none)
      `the day I made the claim` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the claim
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (or the day you will refer the dispute)
      `the dispute is only about matters notified as disputed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether everything in the dispute was set out in the insurer's notice of dispute (or, for a lump sum, raised in writing between you about the offer)
      `the Commission thinks it in the interests of justice to deal with the rest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission is of the opinion that it is in the interests of justice to deal with matters not previously notified
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I refer my dispute to the Commission` MEANS
    IF `the s 289 gate is open`
    THEN IF    `only a failure to decide`
            OR `the dispute is only about matters notified as disputed`
            OR `the Commission thinks it in the interests of justice to deal with the rest`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `disputed` MEANS `the insurer wholly disputes liability` OR `the insurer disputes part of liability`
        `offer a month old` MEANS `the insurer made me an offer of settlement` AND `a month has passed since the offer`
        `a month has passed since the offer` MEANS (`the date` 1 `months after` `the day of the offer`) `is on or before` `today`
        `the s 289 gate is open` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the kind of dispute`
            WHEN `weekly payments` THEN `disputed` OR `the insurer has not decided my claim in time`
            WHEN `weekly payments, on a work capacity decision` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `medical expenses` THEN `disputed` OR `the insurer has not decided my claim in time`
            WHEN `whether proposed treatment is reasonably necessary` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `lump sum compensation` THEN `the insurer wholly disputes liability` OR `offer a month old` OR `the insurer has not decided my claim in time`
            WHEN `property damage` THEN `disputed` OR `28 days have passed since the claim`
        `28 days have passed since the claim` MEANS (`the date` 28 `days after` `the day I made the claim`) `is on or before` `today`
        `only a failure to decide` MEANS `the insurer has not decided my claim in time` AND (NOT `disputed`) AND (NOT `offer a month old`)

-- s 289B(1) referring a dispute about "a work capacity decision to discontinue, or reduce the amount of, weekly payments
-- ... operates to stay the decision"; (2) "only if the dispute is referred ... before the expiry of the required period
-- of notice under section 80." Where s 80 gives no required period (under 12 weeks of payments), the text is silent.
@export Will referring my dispute stop the insurer acting on its work capacity decision to stop or cut my weekly payments (s 289B)?
GIVEN `the day this run of weekly payments began` IS A DATE @desc The first day of the continuous period in which you have been receiving weekly payments
      `the day of the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer gave you notice of the work capacity decision to stop or reduce your payments
      `the day I refer the dispute` IS A DATE @desc The day the dispute is (or will be) referred to the Commission
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Will my referral stay the work capacity decision` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the end of` (`the notice required, run from` `the day this run of weekly payments began` `notice on` `the day of the notice` `work capacity` TRUE `step down` FALSE) `from` `the day of the notice`
    WHEN JUST `the end` THEN
        IF `the day I refer the dispute` `is before` `the end`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 80(2) gives no required period of notice to refer before)

§§ `Interim payment directions (ss 297-304)`

-- s 297(3) "The President is to presume that an interim payment direction for weekly payments of compensation is
-- warranted unless it appears to the President that -- (a) the claim concerned has minimal prospects of success, or (b)
-- the worker has returned to work, or (c) the injury was not reported by the worker as required by section 44 ..., or
-- (d) insufficient medical evidence is available concerning the period of incapacity of the worker, or (e) circumstances
-- ... prescribed by the regulations". Workers Compensation Regulation 2016 cl 42: not presumed "where the insurer has
-- given the worker notice under section 78". (5) the presumption does not limit when a direction can be given.
@export Is an interim payment direction for my weekly payments presumed to be warranted (s 297(3), Regulation cl 42)?
GIVEN `my claim appears to have minimal prospects of success` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the President that your claim has minimal prospects of success
      `I have returned to work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have returned to work
      `I did not report the injury to my employer as soon as possible` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed to notify your employer of the injury as soon as possible, as s 44 requires
      `there is not enough medical evidence of my incapacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether insufficient medical evidence is available about the period of your incapacity
      `the insurer has given me a notice of its decision under s 78` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has given you a notice under s 78 (disputing liability, or stopping or reducing your weekly payments)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is an interim payment direction presumed warranted` MEANS
    IF    `my claim appears to have minimal prospects of success`
       OR `I have returned to work`
       OR `I did not report the injury to my employer as soon as possible`
       OR `there is not enough medical evidence of my incapacity`
       OR `the insurer has given me a notice of its decision under s 78`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 297(4) "If an injury management plan for the worker is in place or the insurer has accepted that the worker has
-- received an injury ..., the President is to presume that an interim payment direction for medical expenses
-- compensation is warranted if satisfied that the treatment or service ... is reasonably necessary -- (a) to prevent
-- deterioration of the worker's condition, or (b) to promote an early return to work, or (c) to relieve significant pain
-- or discomfort".
@export Is an interim payment direction for my medical expenses presumed to be warranted (s 297(4))?
GIVEN `I have an injury management plan, or the insurer accepts I was injured` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an injury management plan is in place for you, or the insurer has accepted that you received an injury
      `the treatment is reasonably necessary to prevent deterioration, help an early return to work or relieve significant pain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied that the treatment or service is reasonably necessary for one of those reasons
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is a medical interim payment direction presumed warranted` MEANS
    IF     `I have an injury management plan, or the insurer accepts I was injured`
       AND `the treatment is reasonably necessary to prevent deterioration, help an early return to work or relieve significant pain`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 297(2) "An interim payment direction for payment of medical expenses compensation cannot be for an amount of more
-- than $7,500 or such other amount as may be prescribed by the regulations." (Note: adjusted under Division 6 of Part 3
-- of the 1987 Act.)
@export What is the most an interim payment direction can make the insurer pay for my medical expenses (s 297(2))?
GIVEN `another amount applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an amount other than the Act's $7,500 applies, prescribed by the regulations or adjusted (indexed) under Division 6 of Part 3 of the 1987 Act
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`The most a medical interim payment direction can give` MEANS
    IF `another amount applies`
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text: the prescribed or indexed amount)
    ELSE JUST 7500

-- s 298(1) a direction for weekly payments "for a period that does not exceed 12 weeks"; (2) "can direct payment of
-- weekly payments during a period that is before the direction is given, but that period must not exceed 10 weeks."
@export Can an interim payment direction cover these weeks of my weekly payments (s 298)?
GIVEN `the day of the direction` IS A DATE @desc The day the interim payment direction is (or would be) given
      `the first day of the period` IS A DATE @desc The first day of the period of weekly payments the direction is to cover
      `the last day of the period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of that period
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can an interim payment direction cover this period` MEANS
    IF     `the days covered` AT MOST 84
       AND `the days before the direction` AT MOST 70
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `the days covered` MEANS (`the days from` `the first day of the period` `to` `the last day of the period`) PLUS 1
        `the days before the direction` MEANS max 0 (`the days from` `the first day of the period` `to` `the day of the direction`)

-- s 304 if the Commission later finds no liability for weekly payments paid under an interim payment direction, "(a) the
-- worker ... is not required to refund those payments unless the Commission otherwise orders under paragraph (b)", which
-- it may where "the claim ... was wholly or partly fraudulent or made without proper justification".
@export Must I pay back weekly payments I received under an interim payment direction (s 304)?
GIVEN `the Commission found the insurer was not liable for them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission has since decided that the insurer was not liable for those weekly payments
      `the Commission ordered me to refund them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission has ordered you to refund all or part of them (because the claim was wholly or partly fraudulent or made without proper justification)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I refund interim payments` MEANS
    IF     `the Commission found the insurer was not liable for them`
       AND `the Commission ordered me to refund them`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 308(1) a party to whom the President recommends action "must, within 14 days (or such longer period as the President
-- may allow ...) -- (a) comply with the recommendation, or (b) request the President to refer the dispute to the
-- Commission"; (2) a worker's failure means "no entitlement to weekly payments of compensation during any period that the
-- failure to comply with the recommendation continues."
@export Do I lose my weekly payments for not following the President's recommendation in a Chapter 3 dispute (s 308)?
GIVEN `the day of the recommendation` IS A DATE @desc The day the President made the recommendation to you
      `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc The days allowed to comply: 14, or the longer period the President allowed
      `I have complied with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have complied with the recommendation
      `I asked for the dispute to go to the Commission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the President, within the time allowed, to refer the dispute to the Commission
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do I lose weekly payments for ignoring a recommendation` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I have complied with it` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `I asked for the dispute to go to the Commission` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `today` `is on or before` (`the date` (max 14 `the days allowed`) `days after` `the day of the recommendation`) THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `Medical assessment of permanent impairment (Part 7)`

-- s 322A(1) "Only one assessment may be made of the degree of permanent impairment of an injured worker." (3) a dispute
-- about it "cannot be referred for, or be the subject of, assessment if a medical dispute about that matter has already
-- been the subject of -- (a) assessment and a medical assessment certificate under this Part, or (b) a determination by
-- the Commission under Part 4." (4) appeals (s 327) and the s 352 appeal are not affected.
@export Can my degree of permanent impairment be assessed for this injury (s 322A)?
GIVEN `it has already been assessed with a medical assessment certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical assessor has already assessed your degree of permanent impairment for this injury and issued a medical assessment certificate
      `the Commission has already determined it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission has already determined your degree of permanent impairment for this injury in a dispute
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my impairment be assessed` MEANS
    IF    `it has already been assessed with a medical assessment certificate`
       OR `the Commission has already determined it`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 323(1) "there is to be a deduction for any proportion of the impairment that is due to any previous injury ... or
-- that is due to any pre-existing condition or abnormality." (2) where its extent "will be difficult or costly to
-- determine ..., it is to be assumed ... that the deduction ... is 10% of the impairment, unless this assumption is at
-- odds with the available evidence." The note's example: 30% less 10% of it is 27%. Percentages are whole numbers here
-- (30 for 30%). A part-deduction under (2) ("or a part of it") is not worked out (fork F10).
@export What is my degree of permanent impairment after the deduction for an earlier injury or pre-existing condition (s 323)?
GIVEN `the assessed degree of permanent impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc Your degree of whole person permanent impairment before the deduction, in percent (for example 30)
      `the share due to an earlier injury or condition` IS A NUMBER @desc The proportion of that impairment due to a previous injury or a pre-existing condition or abnormality, in percent of the impairment (0 if none)
      `that share is difficult or costly to work out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the extent of that deduction will be difficult or costly to determine (for example for lack of medical evidence)
      `10% would be at odds with the evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether assuming a 10% deduction would be at odds with the available medical evidence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`My impairment after the s 323 deduction` MEANS
    IF     `that share is difficult or costly to work out`
       AND (NOT `10% would be at odds with the evidence`)
    THEN `the assessed degree of permanent impairment` TIMES 90 DIVIDED BY 100
    ELSE `the assessed degree of permanent impairment` TIMES (100 MINUS `the share due to an earlier injury or condition`) DIVIDED BY 100

-- s 327(3) the grounds: "(a) deterioration of the worker's condition that results in an increase in the degree of
-- permanent impairment, (b) availability of additional relevant information (but only if [it] was not available to, and
-- could not reasonably have been obtained by, the appellant before the medical assessment ...), (c) the assessment was
-- made on the basis of incorrect criteria, (d) the medical assessment certificate contains a demonstrable error." (5) on
-- (c) or (d) "within 28 days after the medical assessment ..., unless the President is satisfied that special
-- circumstances justify an increase". (7) "no appeal ... once the dispute concerned has been the subject of determination
-- by a court or the Commission or agreement registered under section 66A of the 1987 Act." (4) the President must also be
-- satisfied a ground is made out: that is the input for each ground.
@export May I appeal against the medical assessment of my permanent impairment (s 327)?
GIVEN `my condition has got worse, raising my impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your condition has deteriorated so that your degree of permanent impairment has increased
      `there is new relevant information I could not have had before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether additional relevant information is available that you did not have and could not reasonably have obtained before the assessment
      `the assessment used incorrect criteria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the assessment was made on the basis of incorrect criteria
      `the certificate has a demonstrable error` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the medical assessment certificate contains a demonstrable error
      `the day of the medical assessment` IS A DATE @desc The day of the medical assessment you want to appeal against
      `the day I appeal` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply (or will apply) to the President to appeal
      `the President allows more time for special circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied that special circumstances justify more than 28 days
      `the dispute has been decided by a court or the Commission, or settled by a registered agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the dispute has already been determined by a court or the Commission, or resolved by an agreement registered under s 66A of the 1987 Act
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I appeal the medical assessment` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the dispute has been decided by a court or the Commission, or settled by a registered agreement` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF    `my condition has got worse, raising my impairment`
              OR `there is new relevant information I could not have had before`
           THEN JUST TRUE
           IF    `the assessment used incorrect criteria`
              OR `the certificate has a demonstrable error`
           THEN IF    `the day I appeal` `is on or before` (`the date` 28 `days after` `the day of the medical assessment`)
                   OR `the President allows more time for special circumstances`
                THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

@export By when must I appeal a medical assessment for incorrect criteria or a demonstrable error (s 327(5))?
GIVEN `the day of the medical assessment` IS A DATE @desc The day of the medical assessment
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I appeal the medical assessment` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `the day of the medical assessment`

-- s 330(2) a worker required to be examined "is entitled to recover from the worker's employer, in addition to any
-- compensation otherwise provided -- (a) the amount of any wages lost ..., and (b) the cost to the worker of any fares,
-- travelling expenses and maintenance necessarily and reasonably incurred"; (3) an escort's, if the worker "is not
-- reasonably able to travel unescorted".
@export What can I recover from my employer for attending a medical assessment (s 330(2), (3))?
GIVEN `the wages I lost attending` IS A NUMBER @desc Wages you lost by attending the examination, in dollars
      `my fares, travel and maintenance` IS A NUMBER @desc Your fares, travelling expenses and maintenance necessarily and reasonably incurred, in dollars (private car at the s 64 rate)
      `my escort's fares, travel and maintenance` IS A NUMBER @desc Your escort's fares, travelling expenses and maintenance, in dollars (0 if none)
      `I could not reasonably travel unescorted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were not reasonably able to travel without an escort
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What can I recover for attending a medical assessment` MEANS
    `the wages I lost attending` PLUS `my fares, travel and maintenance` PLUS `the escort's costs`
    WHERE
        `the escort's costs` MEANS
            IF `I could not reasonably travel unescorted`
            THEN `my escort's fares, travel and maintenance`
            ELSE 0

§§ `Appealing a Commission decision (s 352)`

-- s 352(3) "There is no appeal under this section unless the amount of compensation at issue on the appeal is both -- (a)
-- at least $5,000 ..., and (b) at least 20% of the amount awarded in the decision appealed against." (3A) an interlocutory
-- decision only "with the leave of the Commission". (4) "within -- (a) 28 days after the decision appealed against is
-- made, or (b) a longer period determined or allowed ... in accordance with the Commission rules."
@export May I appeal to a presidential member against a decision of a Commission member (s 352)?
GIVEN `the amount of compensation at issue on the appeal` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of compensation in issue on your appeal, in dollars
      `the amount awarded in the decision` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount awarded in the decision you want to appeal against, in dollars (0 if nothing was awarded)
      `the decision is interlocutory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is interlocutory (made along the way, not finally deciding the dispute)
      `the Commission gave me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission has granted you leave to appeal (needed for an interlocutory decision)
      `the day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was made
      `the day I appeal` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply (or will apply) to the President to appeal
      `a longer period is allowed under the Commission rules` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a longer period than 28 days has been determined or allowed under the Commission rules
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I appeal the Commission decision` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the amount of compensation at issue on the appeal` LESS THAN 5000 THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the amount of compensation at issue on the appeal` LESS THAN (`the amount awarded in the decision` TIMES 20 DIVIDED BY 100) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF     `the decision is interlocutory`
              AND (NOT `the Commission gave me leave`)
           THEN JUST FALSE
           IF    `the day I appeal` `is on or before` (`the date` 28 `days after` `the day of the decision`)
              OR `a longer period is allowed under the Commission rules`
           THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§§ `Suing my employer for work injury damages (Chapter 7 Part 6)`

-- s 280B(1) "An injured worker cannot recover damages in respect of an injury from the employer ... unless and until any
-- permanent impairment compensation to which the worker is entitled in respect of the injury has been paid."
@export Does s 280B stop me recovering work injury damages from my employer yet?
GIVEN `all permanent impairment compensation I am entitled to has been paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether every amount of permanent impairment (lump sum) compensation you are entitled to for this injury has been paid (yes if you are entitled to none)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does s 280B stop me recovering damages` MEANS
    IF `all permanent impairment compensation I am entitled to has been paid`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 314(1) a threshold dispute exists if the insurer "(a) has not accepted that the degree of permanent impairment ... is
-- at least 15%, or (b) there is a dispute as to whether the degree ... is fully ascertainable"; (2) none if "(a) [it]
-- has accepted ... at least 15%, or (b) a medical assessor has ... [certified] at least 15%"; (3) acceptance for a
-- permanent impairment compensation claim counts. Where (1)(b) and (2) both hold, (2) is taken to prevail (fork F11).
-- s 313 a claimant with a threshold dispute "cannot commence court proceedings ... and cannot serve a pre-filing
-- statement ... unless the degree of permanent impairment has been assessed by a medical assessor under Part 7." The
-- assessment lifts this bar whatever its result: the 15% threshold for an award of damages itself is s 151H of the 1987
-- Act (finding 3).
GIVEN `the insurer accepts at least 15%` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a medical assessor certified at least 15%` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is disputed whether my impairment is fully ascertainable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the s 313 threshold bar holds` `the insurer accepts at least 15%` `certified` `a medical assessor certified at least 15%` `ascertainable disputed` `it is disputed whether my impairment is fully ascertainable` `assessed` `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment` MEANS
        (NOT `no dispute`)
    AND `a threshold dispute`
    AND (NOT `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment`)
    WHERE
        `no dispute` MEANS `the insurer accepts at least 15%` OR `a medical assessor certified at least 15%`
        `a threshold dispute` MEANS (NOT `the insurer accepts at least 15%`) OR `it is disputed whether my impairment is fully ascertainable`

@export Does the 15% impairment threshold dispute rule stop me serving a pre-filing statement or suing for work injury damages (ss 313, 314)?
GIVEN `the insurer accepts at least 15%` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has accepted that your degree of permanent impairment from the injury is at least 15% (for this claim or for your lump sum compensation claim)
      `a medical assessor certified at least 15%` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical assessor has given a medical assessment certificate of at least 15%
      `it is disputed whether my impairment is fully ascertainable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is a dispute about whether your degree of permanent impairment is fully ascertainable yet
      `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical assessor of the Commission has assessed your degree of permanent impairment (whatever the result)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the threshold dispute stop me` MEANS
    IF `the s 313 threshold bar holds` `the insurer accepts at least 15%` `certified` `a medical assessor certified at least 15%` `ascertainable disputed` `it is disputed whether my impairment is fully ascertainable` `assessed` `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 315(1) a pre-filing statement must be served before proceedings; (2) it "cannot be served unless -- (a) the person on
-- whom the claim is made wholly disputes liability ..., or (b) [it] has made an offer of settlement ... as and when
-- required by section 281 and 1 month has elapsed since the offer was made, or (c) [it] has failed to determine the claim
-- as and when required by section 281." With s 313.
@export May I serve my pre-filing statement on my employer now (ss 313, 315)?
GIVEN `the insurer accepts at least 15%` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has accepted that your degree of permanent impairment is at least 15%
      `a medical assessor certified at least 15%` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical assessor has certified at least 15%
      `it is disputed whether my impairment is fully ascertainable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is a dispute about whether your degree of permanent impairment is fully ascertainable yet
      `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical assessor has assessed your degree of permanent impairment (whatever the result)
      `the insurer wholly disputes liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer (for your employer) wholly disputes liability for your damages claim
      `the insurer made me an offer of settlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer made you an offer of settlement when determining the claim under s 281
      `the day of the offer` IS A DATE @desc The day of that offer (any day if none)
      `the insurer has not decided my claim in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has failed to determine your damages claim as and when s 281 requires (including not making a reasonable offer)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (or the day you will serve)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I serve my pre-filing statement` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the s 313 threshold bar holds` `the insurer accepts at least 15%` `certified` `a medical assessor certified at least 15%` `ascertainable disputed` `it is disputed whether my impairment is fully ascertainable` `assessed` `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment`
           THEN JUST FALSE
           IF    `the insurer wholly disputes liability`
              OR `an offer a month old`
              OR `the insurer has not decided my claim in time`
           THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `an offer a month old` MEANS `the insurer made me an offer of settlement` AND `a month has passed since the offer`
        `a month has passed since the offer` MEANS (`the date` 1 `months after` `the day of the offer`) `is on or before` `today`

-- s 316(1) "The defendant must, within 28 days after the pre-filing statement has been served on the defendant, respond
-- ... by -- (a) accepting or denying liability ..., and (b) ... serving on the claimant a defence"; (2) "If the
-- defendant fails to respond ... within 42 days after it is served ..., the claimant can commence court proceedings";
-- s 318(1)(c) the defendant then cannot file a defence disputing liability; s 318A(2) the claimant need not mediate.
-- The rule below is the 42-day consequence; the 28 days is a plain date. Time 0 is the day of service.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim act`
`s 316 -- the defendant must respond to the pre-filing statement within 42 days, or I may sue without mediation` MEANS
    PARTY `the defendant`
    MUST `respond to the pre-filing statement`
    WITHIN 42
    LEST BREACH BY `the defendant` BECAUSE "no response within 42 days: the claimant may commence court proceedings without mediation, and the defendant cannot file a defence disputing liability (ss 316(2), 318(1)(c), 318A(2))"

GIVEN `the day I served my pre-filing statement` IS A DATE
      `the defendant has responded` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day it responded` IS A DATE
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `What became of the duty`
`what became of the response to a statement served` `the day I served my pre-filing statement` `responded` `the defendant has responded` `on` `the day it responded` `as of` `today` MEANS
    `what became of` `s 316 -- the defendant must respond to the pre-filing statement within 42 days, or I may sue without mediation` `for` `the defendant` `with` `respond to the pre-filing statement` `done` `the defendant has responded` `at` (`day` `the day it responded`) `now` (`day` `today`)
    WHERE
        GIVEN `the date` IS A DATE
        GIVETH A NUMBER
        `day` `the date` MEANS max 0 (`the days from` `the day I served my pre-filing statement` `to` `the date`)

@export By when must my employer respond to my pre-filing statement (s 316(1))?
GIVEN `the day I served my pre-filing statement` IS A DATE @desc The day your pre-filing statement was served on the defendant (the last of its documents, if served at different times)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the defendant respond` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `the day I served my pre-filing statement`

@export From what day may I refer my work injury damages claim to mediation (s 318A(1))?
GIVEN `the day I served my pre-filing statement` IS A DATE @desc The day your pre-filing statement was served on the defendant
GIVETH A DATE
`From when may I refer to mediation` MEANS `the date` 28 `days after` `the day I served my pre-filing statement`

-- s 318A(1) "A claimant must refer a claim for work injury damages for mediation ... before the claimant can commence
-- court proceedings"; (2) not if the defendant failed to respond within 42 days; (4) "cannot be commenced while the claim
-- is the subject of mediation". s 262 a claim must have been made first; s 313 the threshold bar.
@export May I start court proceedings for work injury damages now (ss 262, 313, 316, 318A)?
GIVEN `I have made a claim for work injury damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have made a claim for the damages on your employer or its insurer
      `the insurer accepts at least 15%` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has accepted that your degree of permanent impairment is at least 15%
      `a medical assessor certified at least 15%` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical assessor has certified at least 15%
      `it is disputed whether my impairment is fully ascertainable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is a dispute about whether your degree of permanent impairment is fully ascertainable yet
      `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical assessor has assessed your degree of permanent impairment (whatever the result)
      `I have served my pre-filing statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have served a pre-filing statement on the defendant
      `the day I served my pre-filing statement` IS A DATE @desc The day it was served (any day if not)
      `the defendant has responded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has responded (accepting or denying liability, with a defence to any part not accepted)
      `the day it responded` IS A DATE @desc The day of that response (any day if none)
      `I referred the claim to mediation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have referred the claim to the President for mediation
      `the mediation is over` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the mediation has ended (the mediator has issued a certificate of the final offers, or the defendant declined to take part because it wholly disputes liability)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (or the day you will commence proceedings)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I start court proceedings for damages` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I have made a claim for work injury damages`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the s 313 threshold bar holds` `the insurer accepts at least 15%` `certified` `a medical assessor certified at least 15%` `ascertainable disputed` `it is disputed whether my impairment is fully ascertainable` `assessed` `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment`
           THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `I have served my pre-filing statement`) THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER `what became of the response to a statement served` `the day I served my pre-filing statement` `responded` `the defendant has responded` `on` `the day it responded` `as of` `today`
               WHEN `the duty was not kept` THEN JUST TRUE
               WHEN `the duty is still open` THEN JUST FALSE
               WHEN `the duty was kept` THEN
                   IF     `I referred the claim to mediation`
                      AND `the mediation is over`
                   THEN JUST TRUE
                   ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 318(1)(c) "the defendant is not entitled to file a defence that wholly or partly disputes liability for the claim if
-- the defendant has failed to serve on the claimant a defence to the claim as required by this Division within 42 days
-- after the claimant served the pre-filing statement". While the 42 days run, it still can.
@export Can my employer still dispute liability when I sue for work injury damages (s 318(1)(c))?
GIVEN `the day I served my pre-filing statement` IS A DATE @desc The day your pre-filing statement was served on the defendant
      `the defendant has served a defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has served on you its response with a defence to the claim
      `the day of the defence` IS A DATE @desc The day it was served (any day if none)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my employer still dispute liability` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what became of the response to a statement served` `the day I served my pre-filing statement` `responded` `the defendant has served a defence` `on` `the day of the defence` `as of` `today`
    WHEN `the duty was not kept` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `the duty is still open` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `the duty was kept` THEN JUST TRUE
